FDWM's own: the tags as EWMH desktops, for other programs (a pager, an
external bar, xdotool), after the upstream ewmhtags patch
(https://dwm.suckless.org/patches/ewmhtags/). Like it, the root window gets
_NET_NUMBER_OF_DESKTOPS, _NET_DESKTOP_NAMES, _NET_DESKTOP_VIEWPORT and
_NET_CURRENT_DESKTOP, kept up to date by view and toggleview. Unlike it,
the scratchpads' tags aren't desktops, so there are nine, the current one is
the lowest tag in view (the patch's is the highest bit, a scratchpad's when
one shows), and a view holding nothing but a scratchpad keeps the one
before. Beyond it: each window's _NET_WM_DESKTOP (its lowest tag,
0xFFFFFFFF on all nine, none on a scratchpad), set wherever
preserveonrestart's _NET_CLIENT_INFO is, and requests to change either,
which a pager or `xdotool set_desktop` sends. A swallowed terminal's
properties follow its program's, as it takes back the program's tags when
the program closes: a pager doesn't see it where it was, and a restart
(which reads _NET_CLIENT_INFO) swallows again on the program's tag rather
than the terminal's old one. The man page says so.

diff --git a/dwm.1 b/dwm.1
index 31e42fc..cecbfb1 100644
--- a/dwm.1
+++ b/dwm.1
@@ -26,6 +26,11 @@ Each tag also keeps its own layout, master area size, number of master
 windows and whether the bar shows, and so does the view of all tags; the
 scratchpads have none of their own. They start as configured and last until
 dwm restarts.
+Other programs, such as a pager or a bar of their own, see the tags as EWMH
+desktops, one per tag and named after it: the current desktop is the lowest
+tag in view, and a window's desktop is its lowest tag, or all of them when it
+has every tag. They can switch tags and move windows that way too; the
+scratchpads are on no desktop.
 .P
 A window started from a terminal (st, or another with isterminal in its rule
 in config.h) takes the terminal's place until it closes, and the terminal then
